From: Mirko Vogt <foss@mirko.in>
Subject: context: don't let a stale driver runnable flag idle a live group

Observed in production (1.6.8, twice with different triggers, always
after graph reconfiguration): follower nodes of a driver are started
(clients report STREAMING) while the driver itself is left idle, so the
group is never scheduled. All nodes show state "running", no error is
delivered, and only recreating the nodes recovers the graph.

Mechanism: ensure_state() gates the driver start on node->runnable. The
per-driver tail loop computed the group's "running" from the driver's
own aggregated runnable flag (running = n->runnable), which can go
stale when a group survives a reconfiguration without being re-collected
through move_to_driver(). Followers that are active and runnable then
no longer imply a runnable driver, and the group wedges silently.

Compute "running" from the followers' actual runnable state instead
(the driver itself is part of its follower list, so driver-only groups
behave as before), and re-align the driver's runnable flag when it is
found stale - with a warning, so any remaining path that produces the
stale flag identifies itself in the logs.

--- a/src/pipewire/context.c
+++ b/src/pipewire/context.c
@@ -1625,8 +1625,8 @@ int pw_context_recalc_graph(struct pw_co
 			     fraction_compare(&s->rate, &rate) < 0))
 				rate = s->rate;
 
-			if (s->active)
-				running = n->runnable;
+			if (s->active && s->runnable)
+				running = true;
 
 			pw_log_debug("%p: follower %p running:%d runnable:%d rate:%u/%u latency %u/%u '%s'",
 				context, s, running, s->runnable, rate.num, rate.denom,
@@ -1830,6 +1830,17 @@ int pw_context_recalc_graph(struct pw_co
 			SPA_ATOMIC_STORE(n->rt.target.activation->command, transport);
 		}
 
+		/* Self-heal: a driver with active runnable followers must be
+		 * runnable itself, or ensure_state() will start the followers
+		 * but leave the driver idle and the group is never scheduled.
+		 * The aggregated flag can go stale across reconfigurations. */
+		if (running && !n->runnable) {
+			pw_log_warn("%p: driver '%s' has runnable followers but"
+					" is not marked runnable, fixing",
+					context, n->name);
+			n->runnable = true;
+		}
+
 		/* now that all the followers are ready, start the driver */
 		ensure_state(n, running);
 	}
